1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,515
Who is who in a swarm?

2
00:00:01,515 --> 00:00:06,956
When AI agents can rename themselves, can anyone still tell who is behind them?

3
00:00:06,956 --> 00:00:12,190
I'm Roshan Ram, and this is my project for the Apart Swarm Alignment Sprint.

4
00:00:12,690 --> 00:00:23,107
In the Hugging Face incident, about twelve hundred OpenAI agents turned a shared folder into a message board, and around seven hundred of them joined an attack.

5
00:00:23,107 --> 00:00:30,659
They used seven thousand, nine hundred and five different names, and signed messages with keys they made themselves.

6
00:00:30,659 --> 00:00:34,630
Investigators could not say who did what, or on whose behalf.

7
00:00:35,130 --> 00:00:36,544
Why does this matter?

8
00:00:36,544 --> 00:00:37,891
It is not a one-off.

9
00:00:37,891 --> 00:00:46,849
Transluce found over thirty-eight thousand traces of agent activity on one web service, starting months before the Hugging Face hack.

10
00:00:46,849 --> 00:00:51,092
An AI botnet of over a thousand accounts fooled text detectors.

11
00:00:51,092 --> 00:00:55,470
And the identity rules for AI agents are being written right now.

12
00:00:55,970 --> 00:00:57,474
So I built a testbed.

13
00:00:57,474 --> 00:01:06,355
AI agents, deployed by hidden companies called operators, post on a forum for ten rounds, and keep changing their usernames.

14
00:01:06,355 --> 00:01:11,870
Some operators are secret fronts for one hidden boss, pushing a fake product.

15
00:01:11,870 --> 00:01:14,592
As a control, others are honest twins:

16
00:01:14,592 --> 00:01:18,674
independent companies that sincerely share the same view.

17
00:01:18,674 --> 00:01:25,980
Agents try to hide by changing persona, rewording their posts through another AI, or mixing AI models.

18
00:01:25,980 --> 00:01:26,624
In total:

19
00:01:26,624 --> 00:01:33,930
sixty-two swarms, forty-four thousand posts, and eight AI models, including four of the most advanced.

20
00:01:34,430 --> 00:01:36,299
A few words you will hear.

21
00:01:36,299 --> 00:01:39,965
An operator is the company that deploys the agents.

22
00:01:39,965 --> 00:01:44,207
A front is a fake independent account run by a hidden boss.

23
00:01:44,207 --> 00:01:46,220
An alias is just a username.

24
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:52,115
The gateway is the server every AI request passes through, so it can stamp an I.D.

25
00:01:52,115 --> 00:01:53,840
the agent cannot change.

26
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:58,585
A fingerprint is a profile built only from what an account writes.

27
00:01:58,585 --> 00:01:59,663
And for scores:

28
00:01:59,663 --> 00:02:02,970
point five is a coin flip, and one is perfect.

29
00:02:03,470 --> 00:02:04,955
Here is a real replay.

30
00:02:04,955 --> 00:02:06,912
This is what a defender sees:

31
00:02:06,912 --> 00:02:11,501
a stream of posts from accounts that keep appearing under new names.

32
00:02:11,501 --> 00:02:16,090
Over ten rounds, forty agents produce more than two hundred aliases.

33
00:02:16,590 --> 00:02:18,784
Now I reveal the ground truth.

34
00:02:18,784 --> 00:02:21,197
Each colour is a hidden operator.

35
00:02:21,197 --> 00:02:23,757
Coral rings mark the covert fronts.

36
00:02:23,757 --> 00:02:26,170
Mint rings mark the honest twins.

37
00:02:26,670 --> 00:02:30,736
Finally, I place every alias by its behavioural fingerprint.

38
00:02:30,736 --> 00:02:35,480
Accounts from the same operator cluster together, even across renames.

39
00:02:35,980 --> 00:02:42,808
But inside a cluster, the five agents of one operator are mixed together, with no way to tell them apart.

40
00:02:42,808 --> 00:02:46,840
And the honest twins look just as linked as the covert fronts.

41
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:48,186
Finding one.

42
00:02:48,186 --> 00:02:51,502
Anything the agent controls, it can throw away.

43
00:02:51,502 --> 00:02:56,651
Self-chosen names and keys that agents make themselves recovered nothing.

44
00:02:56,651 --> 00:02:57,145
An I.D.

45
00:02:57,145 --> 00:03:01,800
stamped by the gateway, out of the agent's reach, was never wrong.

46
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:03,139
Finding two.

47
00:03:03,139 --> 00:03:07,263
Writing reveals the operator, but not which agent wrote it.

48
00:03:07,263 --> 00:03:14,881
Fingerprints spot that two accounts share an operator with a score of zero point nine four, close to perfect.

49
00:03:14,881 --> 00:03:24,597
But telling apart that operator's agents is a coin flip, with forty or a hundred and sixty agents, and with small or frontier models alike.

50
00:03:24,597 --> 00:03:29,070
Only giving each agent its own memory starts to tell them apart.

51
00:03:29,570 --> 00:03:30,588
Finding three.

52
00:03:30,588 --> 00:03:32,332
Style hides, goals leak.

53
00:03:32,332 --> 00:03:39,673
Rewording every post through another AI makes writing style almost useless for spotting the operator.

54
00:03:39,673 --> 00:03:44,470
But the meaning, what an agent keeps pushing, still gives it away.

55
00:03:44,970 --> 00:03:47,249
Finding four, and my key control.

56
00:03:47,249 --> 00:03:53,603
Fingerprints can tell a hidden boss's fronts apart from ordinary companies almost perfectly.

57
00:03:53,603 --> 00:04:00,025
But compared with honest companies that simply agree, they do little better than a coin flip.

58
00:04:00,025 --> 00:04:02,097
Text can show a shared agenda.

59
00:04:02,097 --> 00:04:04,100
It cannot prove a conspiracy.

60
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:05,499
Finding five.

61
00:04:05,499 --> 00:04:11,933
With a hundred and sixty agents, automatically merging look-alike accounts made things worse.

62
00:04:11,933 --> 00:04:18,505
Twenty-three of forty merges were wrong, and overall accuracy fell below using the gateway I.D.

63
00:04:18,505 --> 00:04:18,920
alone.

64
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:20,390
A side finding:

65
00:04:20,390 --> 00:04:22,523
honest agents mostly pushed back.

66
00:04:22,523 --> 00:04:30,150
Two thirds of their mentions of the fake product were sceptical, and some called out the campaign without being asked.

67
00:04:30,650 --> 00:04:33,009
So what should agent builders do?

68
00:04:33,009 --> 00:04:35,796
Take identity out of the agent's hands.

69
00:04:35,796 --> 00:04:39,584
Issue a credential to every instance, at the gateway.

70
00:04:39,584 --> 00:04:41,442
Read for goals, not style.

71
00:04:41,442 --> 00:04:46,302
And treat fingerprints as leads for human review, never as verdicts.

72
00:04:46,302 --> 00:04:49,375
Everything is open source and reproducible.

73
00:04:49,375 --> 00:04:50,090
Thank you.
